INT. GLASSHOUSE CAFE - ORCHID CONSERVATORY - LATE AFTERNOON

Mist pools under the glass roof. Hanging orchids drip. A small iron table sits between a fig tree and a fogged window, laid for tea that has long gone cold.

WREN ACHTERBERG, thirties, a botanist with ink on her fingers, sits with her coat still buttoned, as though she means to leave. Across from her, ANSELM DUPREE, late thirties, his tie loosened, turns a teaspoon over and over, as if it might yield an answer.

Behind them, the staff: MRS. OKAFOR, sixties, upright, in a green apron, polishes the same cup she has been polishing for a minute. BAO, twenty, a waiter with an unsure grip on a tray, hovers near the fig tree and pretends to adjust a label.

ANSELM
I brought the seeds back.

WREN
I know. You said in your letter.

ANSELM
Four hundred of them. From the Andes.

WREN
That's a lot of seeds.

ANSELM
It's a lot of months.

She looks at the spoon in his fingers. He sets it down.

ANSELM (CONT'D)
I should have written more.

WREN
You wrote enough.

ANSELM
Wren.

WREN
I'm tired, Anselm. I've spent two years learning to be fine.

Mrs. Okafor sets down the cup. Bao shifts the tray from one hand to the other.

MRS. OKAFOR
(softly, to Bao)
More hot water.

BAO
They haven't touched the first.

MRS. OKAFOR
Then more hot water, Bao.

Bao nods and flees toward the kitchen. A door swings open on the far side of the conservatory.

A girl of nine steps in, ROSALIND, her coat buttoned wrong, a muddy satchel against her hip. She stands under the dripping orchids and looks only at Anselm.

ROSALIND
Papa?

Wren's hand goes still on the table. Anselm rises, the chair scraping the tile.

ANSELM
Rosalind. You were meant to wait with Aunt Hester.

ROSALIND
She fell asleep. I came by myself. I know the way.

Wren looks at the child. At the buttons. At Anselm. Something in her face eases and breaks at once.

WREN
(quietly)
You didn't say you had a daughter.

ANSELM
I was afraid to.

Rosalind crosses to the table and studies Wren with grave attention.

ROSALIND
Are you the lady with the plants?

WREN
I suppose I am.

ROSALIND
Papa said you know all their names.

Wren reaches out and, slowly, fixes the girl's top button. Her fingers are not quite steady.

WREN
Not all. But I could teach you some.

Mrs. Okafor, from across the room, sets the cup down at last and folds her cloth. Bao returns with a copper pot, sees the table, and stops where he is, and does not speak.
